-- Expected (line, col, len, role) for walk.id. HAND-WRITTEN from the law.
--
-- What this fixture discriminates: `.field` versus `.case`, two hues over one
-- glyph. With a left operand, `a.b` is data access (pass100 §4) and is the
-- green `walk` role, decidable, and it carries the card `read` — a walk-WRITE
-- is a different card and is underlined (§0g).
--
-- A LEADING `.name` is the harder half. grammar.md R2 emits ONE node
-- (`anchorref`) and says "resolution is semantic". What IS readable off the
-- surface is the OPERATOR TO THE LEFT: after `=`, `(`, `,` or `|` a descriptor
-- is expected, which is §0.3's inferred case. Line 3's `.eof` sits to the right
-- of `==`, so it is `case` by POSITION. The residual is stated rather than
-- hidden: position is what the projector claims, and case-SET MEMBERSHIP —
-- R2's "against a KNOWN case-set" — remains a graph fact it does not assert.
--
-- At the head of a clause neither reading is available and neither may be
-- defaulted, which is what `fixtures/highlight/mixed/dot.id` proves: R2's own
-- "neither context => diagnostic" and G-TOTAL's "two candidates is a
-- mixed-space DIAGNOSTIC, never a guess" are the same instruction. -- gap[078]
spans = {
    { 1, 1, 7, "comment" },
    { 2, 1, 1, "place" },
    { 2, 3, 1, "operator" },
    { 2, 5, 1, "place" },
    { 2, 6, 4, "walk" },
    { 3, 1, 1, "place" },
    { 3, 3, 1, "operator" },
    { 3, 5, 1, "place" },
    { 3, 7, 2, "operator" },
    { 3, 10, 4, "case" },
}
